## Project context (applies to every task — paste it with your task)

- **App:** SetuAI, a gamified learning app for kids. Frontend project root: `frontend/gamifiedsetu`. All paths in the tasks are relative to it.
- **Stack:** Next.js 16 (App Router), React 19, TypeScript (strict), Tailwind CSS v4, `motion` (Framer Motion's current package; import from `motion/react`). Tests use Vitest + React Testing Library + user-event in jsdom, with jest-dom matchers loaded. Next.js 16 differs from older versions, so check `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` before relying on memory.
- **Imports:** `@/` points to the project root, and there is **no `src/` folder**. For example: `@/types/learning`, `@/api/learningApi`, `@/app/components/learning/...`.
- **Folders:**
  - Shared types go in `types/`.
  - The fake/real API wrappers go in the root-level `api/`.
  - Hooks go in `hooks/`.
  - Components go in `app/components/<feature>/`. Step 4 uses `app/components/common/` (shared building blocks), `app/components/learning/` (learn, challenge, hub) and `app/components/quiz/`. Step 5 uses `app/components/progress/`.
  - Small **pure functions** (same input → same output, no side effects) go in `utils/`, e.g. `utils/levels.ts`.
  - **The two tracks don't import each other's new files.** Step 4 and Step 5 are built at the same time, so a file the other track hasn't merged yet may not exist. The one exception is a plain link to the other track's route (e.g. Step 5 linking to `/module/1`), which is fine.
  - Dev/test pages go in `app/dev/<name>/page.tsx`.
  - Tests sit next to the file they test, as `<Name>.test.ts(x)`.
- **Never create anything under `app/api/`.** In the App Router that folder is for server route handlers.
- **Exports:** components are default exports. Hooks, functions, constants and types are named exports.
- **Client code:** any file that uses state, effects, refs or event handlers starts with `"use client";`.
- **Route pages stay tiny.** Each `page.tsx` is a server component that only reads the route param and renders a client "screen" component. Screen components take `moduleId` as a prop, which makes them easy to test without faking the router. In Next.js 16, `params` is a **Promise**:
  ```tsx
  import LearnScreen from "@/app/components/learning/LearnScreen";

  export default async function LearnPage({ params }: PageProps<"/learn/[moduleId]">) {
    const { moduleId } = await params;
    return <LearnScreen moduleId={moduleId} />;
  }
  ```
  `PageProps` is a global type that Next generates. If `npx tsc --noEmit` says it doesn't know your route, run `npm run dev` once (or `npx next typegen`) so Next generates the route types, then re-run the check.
- **Login:** `proxy.ts` sends logged-out visitors from any non-public page back to `/`. `/module`, `/learn`, `/quiz` and `/challenge` are real app screens, so **sign in before checking them in the browser**. `/dev/...` pages are public. **Don't edit `proxy.ts`.**
- **Backend reality (checked on `main`):** the backend runs locally at `http://127.0.0.1:8000`, all JSON field names are **snake_case** (`module_id`, not `moduleId`), and ids are **integers**.
  - **Exists:**
    - `GET /modules/{id}` returns the module's `id`, `title`, `description` and `order`.
    - `GET /sub-modules/?module_id=` returns sub-module titles and descriptions only, with no lesson content.
    - `POST /submissions` checks challenge answers **on the server** (Step 6 already uses it).
  - **Seed data:** module 1 is "What Is AI?", with four sub-modules: "Swipe-Sort: AI or Not AI?", "AI History Timeline", "Case Study: DeepMind & the NHS" and "Biased Dataset Sandbox". The seeded challenge (id 1 on a fresh database) is "Describe a sunset"; it passes with 8+ words that include "mood" and "style".
  - **Missing:**
    - No lesson-content endpoint.
    - No quiz or answer-check endpoint.
    - No endpoint to fetch a challenge.
    - **No CORS**, so the browser can't call the backend at all yet.
  - **So:** Step 4 is built and finished on mock data. Task 15 sends Backend the list of what we need, and connecting the real backend is a later follow-up (see the end of this file).
- **Styling:** Tailwind with the brand tokens from `app/globals.css` only. Don't invent colours.
  - Colours: `bg-cream`, `bg-coral`, `bg-tealpop`, `bg-white`, `text-ink`, `border-ink`, `text-taskgray`.
  - Font: `font-display`.
  - Card look: `rounded-2xl border-4 border-ink bg-white shadow-[8px_8px_0_#172A32]`.
  - Button look: `rounded-2xl border-4 border-ink font-display font-bold text-ink shadow-[4px_4px_0_#172A32]`. Add a small lift on hover (`transition-transform hover:-translate-y-0.5`), and make it faded with a not-allowed cursor when disabled (`disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-50`). Primary buttons are `bg-coral` or `bg-tealpop`, secondary ones `bg-white`.
